<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-10-31.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="034204"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#034204" label="[03:42:04]">&lt;fotis&gt;</a></span><span class="message">@nore: thanks for sharing on the forth over subleq! 20+ years ago I got the bug of OISCs and eventually picked a subneg variant: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-instruction_set_computer#subneg4">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-instruction_set_computer#subneg4</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="034205"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#034205" label="[03:42:05]">&lt;fotis&gt;</a></span><span class="message">@nore: somewhere earlier in the same page, there is a reference to DawnOS apparently implemented in subleq, interesting!</span></div><div class="line" id="034340"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#034340" label="[03:43:40]">&lt;fotis&gt;</a></span><span class="message">@nore: what were the technicalities of preferring subleq over subneg? It's too far in time to remember what I was doing back then with it, but I recall that early algorithms were better to play with with subneg rather than others of the same family(-ies).</span></div><div class="line" id="035929"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#035929" label="[03:59:29]">&lt;fotis&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also, in case anyone missed it: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://linux.slashdot.org/story/24/09/28/2238229/how-i-booted-linux-on-an-intel-4004-from-1971">https://linux.slashdot.org/story/24/09/28/2238229/how-i-booted-linux-on-an-intel-4004-from-1971</a>  (linux over MIPS over 4004); for the record, the schematics for 4004 were released in the wild by Intel around year 2006 - not that it should be too hard to put it together from scratch. @nore: On other news, I recall that</span></div><div class="line" id="035929"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#035929" label="[03:59:29]">&lt;fotis&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the decoding unit for subneg4 cpu was a very sparse 12x8 matrix which was implementable with a handful of TTL chips, and cascaded EPROMs for the ALU unit. The subleq implementation should be about the same complexity, so there you go!</span></div><div class="line" id="094318"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#094318" label="[09:43:18]">&lt;[exa]&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Hi all</span></div><div class="line" id="094344"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#094344" label="[09:43:44]">&lt;[exa]&gt;</a></span><span class="message">is there some language design/theoretical reading about M0? I read it's a pretty cool macro system but kinda searching for what the limits are</span></div><div class="line" id="100734"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#100734" label="[10:07:34]">&lt;[exa]&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(in particular, can the macros trigger evaluation of other macros?)</span></div><div class="line" id="101149"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#101149" label="[10:11:49]">&lt;[exa]&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(ok I assume not, finding there's also M1 and M2 :D )</span></div><div class="line" id="101649"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#101649" label="[10:16:49]">&lt;ekaitz&gt;</a></span><span class="message">[exa]: M0 is just ELF files written as hex strings</span></div><div class="line" id="101722"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#101722" label="[10:17:22]">&lt;ekaitz&gt;</a></span><span class="message">[exa]: there's nothing really special about them (apart from the fact that someone had to come up with the idea)</span></div><div class="line" id="104301"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#104301" label="[10:43:01]">&lt;[exa]&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ekaitz: isn't that hex0?</span></div><div class="line" id="113919"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#113919" label="[11:39:19]">&lt;ekaitz&gt;</a></span><span class="message">[exa]: Yes, i'm an idiot</span></div><div class="line" id="113951"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#113951" label="[11:39:51]">&lt;ekaitz&gt;</a></span><span class="message">[exa]: sorry :(</span></div><div class="line" id="141622"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#141622" label="[14:16:22]">&lt;[exa]&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ekaitz: ah np :D</span></div><div class="line" id="141649"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#141649" label="[14:16:49]">&lt;[exa]&gt;</a></span><span class="message">like, long story short, I'm bootstrapping haskell via a small vm that I've got in assembly</span></div><div class="line" id="141714"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#141714" label="[14:17:14]">&lt;[exa]&gt;</a></span><span class="message">made up an intermediate forth-like language, so kinda wondering if M0 or M1 would on itself be able to translate it</span></div><div class="line" id="143552"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#143552" label="[14:35:52]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">not an urgent question: which and if any was the reason for perl-5.6.2 instead of perl-5.8.6 (at the bootstrapping stage of perl with tcc/static)</span></div><div class="line" id="143650"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#143650" label="[14:36:50]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'm re-iterating over perl again, and 5.8.6 was the last-known that i could compile/link with tcc statically (except for some extensions that i missed for automake/autotools last time i tried)</span></div><div class="line" id="144006"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#144006" label="[14:40:06]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i am not worried if i either perl 5.6.2 or 5.8.6 were salvaged for autotools support</span></div><div class="line" id="144157"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#144157" label="[14:41:57]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i've just had perl on the hitlist of troublemakers for a little while, and rather avoided any further problems</span></div><div class="line" id="152902"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#152902" label="[15:29:02]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; [exa]: M0 is basically just string substitution with hex</span></div><div class="line" id="152914"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#152914" label="[15:29:14]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; Well, with ASCII form of hex</span></div><div class="line" id="152934"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#152934" label="[15:29:34]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; Plus some support for encoding immediate constants...</span></div><div class="line" id="153004"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#153004" label="[15:30:04]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; Macros can't directly trigger other  macros, it is one pass assembler</span></div><div class="line" id="153021"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#153021" label="[15:30:21]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; You could create something where you run M0 twice</span></div><div class="line" id="153028"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#153028" label="[15:30:28]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; But we didn't do that anywhere</span></div><div class="line" id="153106"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#153106" label="[15:31:06]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; aggi: cause 5.6 was sufficient for our purposes</span></div><div class="line" id="153127"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#153127" label="[15:31:27]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; We had to bootstrap pregenerated files from earlier versions of perl</span></div><div class="line" id="153135"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#153135" label="[15:31:35]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; So 5.8 would be next step</span></div><div class="line" id="153554"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#153554" label="[15:35:54]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ok, stikonas, then that's the difference: i picked the latest version possible with tcc, bootstrappable picked the minimum version, without any other side-effect known</span></div><div class="line" id="185548"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#185548" label="[18:55:48]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">trying to re-produce steps/perl-5.6.2 i'm digging into live-bootstrap currently, git-synced including all latest submodules</span></div><div class="line" id="185649"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#185649" label="[18:56:49]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">trivial question: is the current live-bootstrap version and it's submodules a known good baseline? there seems no git tags available for any.</span></div><div class="line" id="190044"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#190044" label="[19:00:44]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">another minor difficulty i am having, identifying how the bundled files config.h,config.sh and main.mk are merged into the perl-5.6 source tree during pass1.sh</span></div><div class="line" id="190103"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#190103" label="[19:01:03]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's not pass1.sh script itself which is copying those files into the perl source tree</span></div><div class="line" id="190433"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#190433" label="[19:04:33]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i spotted the script-configurator.c already, that processes the steps/manifest; nonetheless it's a littly mysterious at first glance, to grasp the sequential logic of perl-5.6 step for example</span></div><div class="line" id="190635"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#190635" label="[19:06:35]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i am not prepared yet to follow complete bootstrapping, but instead salvage parts to move those into the system-profile for tcc-toolchain support</span></div><div class="line" id="190835"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#190835" label="[19:08:35]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i see, steps/helper.sh, however that's related to steps/manifest interpreted by script-generator.c, i'll find out</span></div><div class="line" id="191811"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#191811" label="[19:18:11]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">currently the tcc profile of mine is locked and trapped inside gentoo ebuilds/portage/crossdev still</span></div><div class="line" id="191848"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#191848" label="[19:18:48]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">bootstrappable scripting seems a decent candidate to converge and re-integrate with, to emit a complete tcc distribution</span></div><div class="line" id="195958"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#195958" label="[19:59:58]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;cosinusoidally&gt; aggi: live-bootstrap has ci the latest commit on master is passing <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/commits/master/">https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/commits/master/</a>  . There was also some talk of a 1.0 release <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/commits/1.0">https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/commits/1.0</a>  but I suspect that may currently be broken due to some ssl url change.</span></div><div class="line" id="200314"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#200314" label="[20:03:14]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">thanks, good to know master branch is mostly stable to rely upon.</span></div><div class="line" id="221841"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#221841" label="[22:18:41]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; cosinusoidally: that SSL issue is workarounded</span></div><div class="line" id="221857"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#221857" label="[22:18:57]">&lt;mihi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">[exa], multiple passes of M0 (constant number of them) are definitely possible, I used them in a project before but ultimately did not publish the result for reasons unrelated to M0. The tricky thing is to quote parts (in double quotes) that you want the second pass to evaluate so that the first pass does not mangle/touch them.</span></div><div class="line" id="222030"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#222030" label="[22:20:30]">&lt;mihi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also, I assume you are aware of <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/blynn/compiler">https://github.com/blynn/compiler</a>  and/or oriansj's fork of it</span></div><div class="line" id="222031"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#222031" label="[22:20:31]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: and for sequential logic of each package, you should look at helpers.sh</span></div><div class="line" id="222034"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#222034" label="[22:20:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">not at manifest</span></div><div class="line" id="222039"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#222039" label="[22:20:39]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">manifest just shows the order of packages</span></div><div class="line" id="222117"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#222117" label="[22:21:17]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/blob/749b2bfe37ae4f0fd03d7b211d35fd34b419d13e/steps/helpers.sh#L208">https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/blob/749b2bfe37ae4f0fd03d7b211d35fd34b419d13e/steps/helpers.sh#L208</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="222225"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#222225" label="[22:22:25]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it just runs functions called src_get, src_unpack src_prepare src_configure src_compile src_install src_postprocess src_pkg src_checksum src_apply</span></div><div class="line" id="222305"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#222305" label="[22:23:05]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">many functions have sane defaults , e.g. src_get <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/blob/749b2bfe37ae4f0fd03d7b211d35fd34b419d13e/steps/helpers.sh#L287">https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/blob/749b2bfe37ae4f0fd03d7b211d35fd34b419d13e/steps/helpers.sh#L287</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="222326"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#222326" label="[22:23:26]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and each package can override them as needed</span></div><div class="line" id="222435"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#222435" label="[22:24:35]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">e.g default_src_prepare applies all patches, copies mk/main.mk file into the unpacked tree and copies everything from files/</span></div><div class="line" id="222546"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#222546" label="[22:25:46]">&lt;[exa]&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas, mihi: thanks for the hints, I guess I'm getting into the logic of the whole structure</span></div><div class="line" id="222619"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#222619" label="[22:26:19]">&lt;[exa]&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tbh I was thinking if I could beat something like primitive register allocation out of it, turns out yes but better not :D</span></div><div class="line" id="222838"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#222838" label="[22:28:38]">&lt;[exa]&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, mihi: yeah I'm aware of blynn's compiler and MicroHS, I'm literally having a small quest of &quot;how this would look without the C in the middle&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="222918"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#222918" label="[22:29:18]">&lt;mihi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ah ok. feel free to follow that quest if you enjoy it :)</span></div><div class="line" id="223028"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#223028" label="[22:30:28]">&lt;[exa]&gt;</a></span><span class="message">there's a few funny minimal evaluators for haskell that are entirely un-C-ish, so trying to patch up something between full haskell and these to make it practical</span></div><div class="line" id="223259"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#223259" label="[22:32:59]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for haskell the hard bit I guess is reaching ghc</span></div><div class="line" id="223317"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#223317" label="[22:33:17]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">smaller haskell compilers can be built  I think</span></div><div class="line" id="223335"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#223335" label="[22:33:35]">&lt;[exa]&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah absolutely, ghc is an enormous single peak</span></div><div class="line" id="223402"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#223402" label="[22:34:02]">&lt;[exa]&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you can get 99% of the things with even smaller compiler than the MicroHS</span></div><div class="line" id="223448"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#223448" label="[22:34:48]">&lt;[exa]&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I don't like the combinator-based runtimes of these though, so trying the good old STG-directly-to-assembly</span></div><div class="line" id="223727"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#223727" label="[22:37:27]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: noted, found this helper.sh already; currently stuffing the perl-5.6.2/pass1.sh into a gentoo ebuild for local testing; seems bison-3.6.4 output isn't compatible with whatever bootstrappable expects to build perl (bison-2.x it seems)</span></div><div class="line" id="223742"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#223742" label="[22:37:42]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which raises another concern, what's considered &quot;human readable&quot; source code</span></div><div class="line" id="223755"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#223755" label="[22:37:55]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, that depends on whom you ask</span></div><div class="line" id="223806"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#223806" label="[22:38:06]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">live-bootstrap rebuilds all bison .y and flex .l files  too</span></div><div class="line" id="223809"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#223809" label="[22:38:09]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">most distros don't</span></div><div class="line" id="223846"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#223846" label="[22:38:46]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">as first pass, you can just try without re-running bison or regenerating header files</span></div><div class="line" id="223914"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#223914" label="[22:39:14]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">perly.c emitted by bison-3.6.4 freaks out as follows (with a little more debug output from gcc): perly.tab.c:1681:13: error: unknown type name 'yysymbol_kind_t'</span></div><div class="line" id="223944"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#223944" label="[22:39:44]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'm not seeking advice on this, it is what it is</span></div><div class="line" id="223959"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#223959" label="[22:39:59]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'll give bison-2.x a try</span></div><div class="line" id="224313"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#224313" label="[22:43:13]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, I wouldn't be surprised</span></div><div class="line" id="225932"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#225932" label="[22:59:32]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's three issues at once: (1) retaining perl/autotools support (2) bison-version required and, (3) related the readability of this</span></div><div class="line" id="230021"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#230021" label="[23:00:21]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">without regenerating, ... y.tab.c:1463: error: macro 'yyparse' used with too many args ... wherever that's coming from since i don't see any call to yacc perly.y anywhere in the Makefile</span></div><div class="line" id="230029"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#230029" label="[23:00:29]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, too much</span></div><div class="line" id="230232"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#230232" label="[23:02:32]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'll give re-generating with bison-2.x a try</span></div><div class="line" id="231110"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#231110" label="[23:11:10]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/blob/749b2bfe37ae4f0fd03d7b211d35fd34b419d13e/steps/perl-5.6.2/pass1.sh#L12">https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/blob/749b2bfe37ae4f0fd03d7b211d35fd34b419d13e/steps/perl-5.6.2/pass1.sh#L12</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="231542"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#231542" label="[23:15:42]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yep, already deactivated regenerating, and the build errored out later then</span></div><div class="line" id="231605"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#231605" label="[23:16:05]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">re-generating with bison-2.3 passes, and a few other issues following then</span></div><br /></div></body></html>